text:
Napoleon Bonaparte was born in 1769 in Ajaccio, in Corsica, into a family of minor nobility. At the age of 9, he left for military school in France. The other pupils made fun of his Corsican accent, but he worked very hard, especially at mathematics.

He became an artillery officer. The Revolution gave him the chance to rise very fast. At 24, he was already a general! In 1796, he led the French army in Italy and won victory after victory. Then he went on an expedition to Egypt, with scientists who studied the pyramids.

He became very popular. In 1799, France was tired of the unrest of the Revolution. Bonaparte took power by force, in a [coup d'état]. He became First Consul.

He brought back order and made many lasting reforms:
- the Civil Code, in 1804, a big book gathering the laws of daily life: marriage, property, inheritance... It is still the basis of French law, and many countries have copied it;
- the [lycées], to train young people;
- the Banque de France and a stable currency, the franc;
- the prefects, who represent the state in each department;
- the Legion of Honour, a decoration to reward people who have served France.

On the 2nd of December 1804, in Notre-Dame de Paris, Napoleon was crowned [emperor]. The Pope was there, but Napoleon placed the crown on his own head himself! Then he crowned his wife, Joséphine. The painter David painted this huge scene.

Napoleon went to war against almost all the kings of Europe. He won famous battles, like Austerlitz in 1805, his greatest victory. His empire stretched from Spain to Poland, and he placed his brothers on the thrones of several countries.

But these wars also caused hundreds of thousands of deaths. And in 1802, he brought back [slavery] in the colonies, which the Revolution had abolished in 1794.

In the 19th century, the countries of Europe competed to conquer territories on other continents. They wanted raw materials, markets to sell their products, and power. This is called [colonisation].

France already had a few old colonies, in the Caribbean and in the Indian Ocean. In 1830, it began the conquest of Algeria. The war was long and very violent: the Algerians, led by Emir Abdelkader, resisted for years.

Then, especially after 1880, France conquered huge territories: most of West Africa and Central Africa, Madagascar, Tunisia, Morocco, but also Indochina (today Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia), as well as islands in the Pacific.

In 1914, the French colonial empire was the second-largest in the world, after the British Empire: more than 20 times the size of France!

The colonisers said they were bringing "civilisation": roads, railways, hospitals, schools. Some doctors and teachers did devote their lives to the local populations. But the reality was mostly harsh and unjust:
- the conquests caused many deaths and destroyed villages;
- the land was often taken from the inhabitants and given to settlers;
- the colonised people were forced to work, sometimes without pay, to build roads and railways;
- they did not have the same rights as French citizens: they could not vote and were subject to special rules;
- their cultures and languages were often despised.

Many French people at the time believed that Europeans were superior to other peoples. It was [racism], and today we know it is false: all human beings are equal.

The colonised peoples never stopped resisting. After the Second World War, they fought for their [independence]. Some wars were terrible, like the one in Indochina and the Algerian War. Most of the colonies became independent countries between 1954 and 1962.

Today, the history of colonisation is still important to understand the links between France and many countries, and the diversity of French society.
